Watch out: Use had been + -ing only when the sentence tells you how long the action had continued (for three hours).', '[{"label":"When to use","text":"To describe an action that was in progress at a particular moment in the past, often when something else happened."},{"label":"Look for","text":"at 9 p.m. last night, at that moment, while…, when the phone rang."},{"label":"Example","text":"I was studying for the test when the power went out."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"Use had been + -ing only when the sentence tells you how long the action had continued (for three hours)."}]', 228);
INSERT INTO feedback_items (feedback_code, test_id, test_part_id, question_id, kind, type_ref, heading, body, fields_json, sort_order) VALUES ('01-01-01-002-29-00', 1, 1, (SELECT id FROM questions WHERE question_code = '01-01-01-002-29'), 'micro_feedback', 'A01-T04', 'Past Perfect (had + past participle)', 'When to use: To show that one past action happened before another past action or time.
Look for: by 10 p.m., before she called, by the time the meeting started, already.
Example: By the time the meeting started, I had finished the report.
Watch out: If events are told in the order they happened, the simple past is usually enough (I finished the report and went home). Use had + past participle when you need to show which action happened first.', '[{"label":"When to use","text":"To show that one past action happened before another past action or time."},{"label":"Look for","text":"by 10 p.m., before she called, by the time the meeting started, already."},{"label":"Example","text":"By the time the meeting started, I had finished the report."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"If events are told in the order they happened, the simple past is usually enough (I finished the report and went home). Use had + past participle when you need to show which action happened first."}]', 229);
INSERT INTO feedback_items (feedback_code, test_id, test_part_id, question_id, kind, type_ref, heading, body, fields_json, sort_order) VALUES ('01-01-01-002-30-00', 1, 1, (SELECT id FROM questions WHERE question_code = '01-01-01-002-30'), 'micro_feedback', 'A01-T05', 'Past Perfect Continuous (had been + -ing)', 'When to use: To show how long an action had continued up to a point in the past.
Look for: a past time or event (when she called, before the train arrived) together with a length of time (for two hours, for three months).
Example: By the time the train arrived, I had been waiting for two hours.
Watch out: Was/were + -ing only shows that an action was in progress, and had + past participle focuses on a finished action. Neither shows how long the action had lasted.', '[{"label":"When to use","text":"To show how long an action had continued up to a point in the past."},{"label":"Look for","text":"a past time or event (when she called, before the train arrived) together with a length of time (for two hours, for three months)."},{"label":"Example","text":"By the time the train arrived, I had been waiting for two hours."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"Was/were + -ing only shows that an action was in progress, and had + past participle focuses on a finished action. Neither shows how long the action had lasted."}]', 230);
INSERT INTO feedback_items (feedback_code, test_id, test_part_id, question_id, kind, type_ref, heading, body, fields_json, sort_order) VALUES ('01-01-01-003-01-00', 1, 1, (SELECT id FROM questions WHERE question_code = '01-01-01-003-01'), 'micro_feedback', 'A01-T07', 'Simple Present', 'When to use: For facts, habits, routines and situations that are generally true, including the findings of a study.
Look for: always, usually, often, rarely, every day, and verbs such as know, believe, seem and contain.
Example: The study shows that regular study habits lead to higher scores.
Watch out: Verbs that describe states, thoughts or feelings (know, believe, own, contain) are not normally used with -ing: I believe…, not I am believing….', '[{"label":"When to use","text":"For facts, habits, routines and situations that are generally true, including the findings of a study."},{"label":"Look for","text":"always, usually, often, rarely, every day, and verbs such as know, believe, seem and contain."},{"label":"Example","text":"The study shows that regular study habits lead to higher scores."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"Verbs that describe states, thoughts or feelings (know, believe, own, contain) are not normally used with -ing: I believe…, not I am believing…."}]', 301);
INSERT INTO feedback_items (feedback_code, test_id, test_part_id, question_id, kind, type_ref, heading, body, fields_json, sort_order) VALUES ('01-01-01-003-02-00', 1, 1, (SELECT id FROM questions WHERE question_code = '01-01-01-003-02'), 'micro_feedback', 'A01-T10', 'Present Perfect Continuous (has/have been + -ing)', 'When to use: To focus on how long an activity has been going on from the past until now, or on an activity that has just stopped and still has visible results.
Look for: for six hours, all morning, since 8 a.m., lately, recently.
Example: The board has been reviewing the reports all morning.
Watch out: When the focus is on a result or on how many things are finished, use has/have + past participle: She has written three reports, not She has been writing three reports.', '[{"label":"When to use","text":"To focus on how long an activity has been going on from the past until now, or on an activity that has just stopped and still has visible results."},{"label":"Look for","text":"for six hours, all morning, since 8 a.m., lately, recently."},{"label":"Example","text":"The board has been reviewing the reports all morning."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"When the focus is on a result or on how many things are finished, use has/have + past participle: She has written three reports, not She has been writing three reports."}]', 302);
INSERT INTO feedback_items (feedback_code, test_id, test_part_id, question_id, kind, type_ref, heading, body, fields_json, sort_order) VALUES ('01-01-01-003-03-00', 1, 1, (SELECT id FROM questions WHERE question_code = '01-01-01-003-03'), 'micro_feedback', 'A01-T07', 'Simple Present', 'When to use: For facts, habits, routines and situations that are generally true, including the findings of a study.
Look for: always, usually, often, rarely, every day, and verbs such as know, believe, seem and contain.
Example: The study shows that regular study habits lead to higher scores.
Watch out: Verbs that describe states, thoughts or feelings (know, believe, own, contain) are not normally used with -ing: I believe…, not I am believing….', '[{"label":"When to use","text":"For facts, habits, routines and situations that are generally true, including the findings of a study."},{"label":"Look for","text":"always, usually, often, rarely, every day, and verbs such as know, believe, seem and contain."},{"label":"Example","text":"The study shows that regular study habits lead to higher scores."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"Verbs that describe states, thoughts or feelings (know, believe, own, contain) are not normally used with -ing: I believe…, not I am believing…."}]', 303);
INSERT INTO feedback_items (feedback_code, test_id, test_part_id, question_id, kind, type_ref, heading, body, fields_json, sort_order) VALUES ('01-01-01-003-04-00', 1, 1, (SELECT id FROM questions WHERE question_code = '01-01-01-003-04'), 'micro_feedback', 'A01-T08', 'Present Continuous (am/is/are + -ing)', 'When to use: For actions happening now or temporary situations around the present.
Look for: now, at the moment, currently, these days, Look!, Listen!
Example: The research team is conducting an experiment in the laboratory today.
Watch out: State verbs are not normally used with -ing: This test contains 60 questions, not This test is containing 60 questions.', '[{"label":"When to use","text":"For actions happening now or temporary situations around the present."},{"label":"Look for","text":"now, at the moment, currently, these days, Look!, Listen!"},{"label":"Example","text":"The research team is conducting an experiment in the laboratory today."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"State verbs are not normally used with -ing: This test contains 60 questions, not This test is containing 60 questions."}]', 304);
INSERT INTO feedback_items (feedback_code, test_id, test_part_id, question_id, kind, type_ref, heading, body, fields_json, sort_order) VALUES ('01-01-01-003-05-00', 1, 1, (SELECT id FROM questions WHERE question_code = '01-01-01-003-05'), 'micro_feedback', 'A01-T09', 'Present Perfect (has/have + past participle)', 'When to use: For past actions that matter now, life experiences, and situations that started in the past and continue now.
Look for: already, yet, ever, never, just, recently, so far, since 2020, for three years.
Example: The department has updated the curriculum to meet new standards.
Watch out: Do not use it with a finished past time such as yesterday or last week: I finished the report yesterday, not I have finished the report yesterday.', '[{"label":"When to use","text":"For past actions that matter now, life experiences, and situations that started in the past and continue now."},{"label":"Look for","text":"already, yet, ever, never, just, recently, so far, since 2020, for three years."},{"label":"Example","text":"The department has updated the curriculum to meet new standards."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"Do not use it with a finished past time such as yesterday or last week: I finished the report yesterday, not I have finished the report yesterday."}]', 305);
