Watch out: Use can for things that are generally possible (Winters in London can be cold). For a guess about one particular situation, use may, might or could: It might be in my pocket, not It can be in my pocket. Note also that might not means “possibly not”, but can’t / couldn’t means “certainly not”.', '[{"label":"When to use","text":"To say that something is possibly true when you are not sure."},{"label":"Look for","text":"I’m not sure, perhaps, maybe, it’s possible."},{"label":"Examples","text":"I’m not sure where my keys are. They may be in the kitchen. Don’t throw away that laptop – it might still work if we replace the battery."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"Use can for things that are generally possible (Winters in London can be cold). For a guess about one particular situation, use may, might or could: It might be in my pocket, not It can be in my pocket. Note also that might not means “possibly not”, but can’t / couldn’t means “certainly not”."}]', 725);
