Key difference: therefore / consequently / as a result = so; otherwise = if not.
Example: Hurry up; otherwise, we will miss the bus.
Watch out: Use otherwise for ‘if not’, not therefore: Leave now; otherwise you''ll be late.', '[{"label":"When to use","text":"To show that one thing is the result of another."},{"label":"Key difference","text":"therefore / consequently / as a result = so; otherwise = if not."},{"label":"Example","text":"Hurry up; otherwise, we will miss the bus."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"Use otherwise for ‘if not’, not therefore: Leave now; otherwise you''ll be late."}]', 2519);
INSERT INTO feedback_items (feedback_code, test_id, test_part_id, question_id, kind, type_ref, heading, body, fields_json, sort_order) VALUES ('01-01-05-025-20-00', 1, 1, (SELECT id FROM questions WHERE question_code = '01-01-05-025-20'), 'micro_feedback', 'A05-T24', 'Short time phrases (while / after / before / upon + -ing)', 'When to use: To shorten a time clause when both parts have the same subject (While I was waiting → While waiting).
Look for: while, after, before, on or upon followed by an -ing form.
Example: Upon arriving at the hotel, we checked in immediately.
Watch out: The person after the comma must be the one doing the action: While waiting for the bus, I saw a friend, not While waiting for the bus, a friend saw me.', '[{"label":"When to use","text":"To shorten a time clause when both parts have the same subject (While I was waiting → While waiting)."},{"label":"Look for","text":"while, after, before, on or upon followed by an -ing form."},{"label":"Example","text":"Upon arriving at the hotel, we checked in immediately."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"The person after the comma must be the one doing the action: While waiting for the bus, I saw a friend, not While waiting for the bus, a friend saw me."}]', 2520);
INSERT INTO feedback_items (feedback_code, test_id, test_part_id, question_id, kind, type_ref, heading, body, fields_json, sort_order) VALUES ('01-01-05-025-21-00', 1, 1, (SELECT id FROM questions WHERE question_code = '01-01-05-025-21'), 'micro_feedback', 'A05-T21', 'Contrast (however, nevertheless, in contrast, on the other hand)', 'When to use: To show a contrast or an unexpected result between two sentences.
Key difference: however / nevertheless = an unexpected result; in contrast / on the other hand = a comparison of two things.
Example: The test was difficult. Nevertheless, most students passed.
Watch out: however links sentences, not clauses: It was late. However, we stayed, not It was late however we stayed.', '[{"label":"When to use","text":"To show a contrast or an unexpected result between two sentences."},{"label":"Key difference","text":"however / nevertheless = an unexpected result; in contrast / on the other hand = a comparison of two things."},{"label":"Example","text":"The test was difficult. Nevertheless, most students passed."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"however links sentences, not clauses: It was late. However, we stayed, not It was late however we stayed."}]', 2521);
INSERT INTO feedback_items (feedback_code, test_id, test_part_id, question_id, kind, type_ref, heading, body, fields_json, sort_order) VALUES ('01-01-05-025-22-00', 1, 1, (SELECT id FROM questions WHERE question_code = '01-01-05-025-22'), 'micro_feedback', 'A05-T23', 'instead vs instead of', 'When to use: To show that one thing replaces another.
Key difference: instead of + noun / -ing (instead of driving); instead stands alone, usually at the end or start of a clause.
Example: We didn''t drive. We took the train instead.
Watch out: instead of taking the bus, not instead taking the bus.', '[{"label":"When to use","text":"To show that one thing replaces another."},{"label":"Key difference","text":"instead of + noun / -ing (instead of driving); instead stands alone, usually at the end or start of a clause."},{"label":"Example","text":"We didn''t drive. We took the train instead."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"instead of taking the bus, not instead taking the bus."}]', 2522);
INSERT INTO feedback_items (feedback_code, test_id, test_part_id, question_id, kind, type_ref, heading, body, fields_json, sort_order) VALUES ('01-01-05-025-23-00', 1, 1, (SELECT id FROM questions WHERE question_code = '01-01-05-025-23'), 'micro_feedback', 'A05-T24', 'Short time phrases (while / after / before / upon + -ing)', 'When to use: To shorten a time clause when both parts have the same subject (While I was waiting → While waiting).
Look for: while, after, before, on or upon followed by an -ing form.
Example: Upon arriving at the hotel, we checked in immediately.
Watch out: The person after the comma must be the one doing the action: While waiting for the bus, I saw a friend, not While waiting for the bus, a friend saw me.', '[{"label":"When to use","text":"To shorten a time clause when both parts have the same subject (While I was waiting → While waiting)."},{"label":"Look for","text":"while, after, before, on or upon followed by an -ing form."},{"label":"Example","text":"Upon arriving at the hotel, we checked in immediately."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"The person after the comma must be the one doing the action: While waiting for the bus, I saw a friend, not While waiting for the bus, a friend saw me."}]', 2523);
INSERT INTO feedback_items (feedback_code, test_id, test_part_id, question_id, kind, type_ref, heading, body, fields_json, sort_order) VALUES ('01-01-05-025-24-00', 1, 1, (SELECT id FROM questions WHERE question_code = '01-01-05-025-24'), 'micro_feedback', 'A05-T27', 'Linking words yet and so', 'When to use: To join two complete sentences. yet = but surprisingly; so = therefore.
Look for: a comma between two full clauses.
Example: He studied hard, yet he failed the test.
Watch out: Do not use yet or so together with although or because in the same sentence.', '[{"label":"When to use","text":"To join two complete sentences. yet = but surprisingly; so = therefore."},{"label":"Look for","text":"a comma between two full clauses."},{"label":"Example","text":"He studied hard, yet he failed the test."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"Do not use yet or so together with although or because in the same sentence."}]', 2524);
INSERT INTO feedback_items (feedback_code, test_id, test_part_id, question_id, kind, type_ref, heading, body, fields_json, sort_order) VALUES ('01-01-05-025-25-00', 1, 1, (SELECT id FROM questions WHERE question_code = '01-01-05-025-25'), 'micro_feedback', 'A05-T26', 'Past participle openers (Startled by…, Designed by…)', 'When to use: To start a sentence with a passive idea about the subject (Startled by the noise = because she was startled).
Look for: a sentence opener that describes what happened to the subject.
Example: Startled by the noise, the cat jumped off the sofa.
Watch out: The subject after the comma must be the one affected: Designed by top engineers, the bridge survived the storm, not Designed by top engineers, the storm hit the bridge.', '[{"label":"When to use","text":"To start a sentence with a passive idea about the subject (Startled by the noise = because she was startled)."},{"label":"Look for","text":"a sentence opener that describes what happened to the subject."},{"label":"Example","text":"Startled by the noise, the cat jumped off the sofa."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"The subject after the comma must be the one affected: Designed by top engineers, the bridge survived the storm, not Designed by top engineers, the storm hit the bridge."}]', 2525);
INSERT INTO feedback_items (feedback_code, test_id, test_part_id, question_id, kind, type_ref, heading, body, fields_json, sort_order) VALUES ('01-01-05-025-26-00', 1, 1, (SELECT id FROM questions WHERE question_code = '01-01-05-025-26'), 'micro_feedback', 'A05-T21', 'Contrast (however, nevertheless, in contrast, on the other hand)', 'When to use: To show a contrast or an unexpected result between two sentences.
Key difference: however / nevertheless = an unexpected result; in contrast / on the other hand = a comparison of two things.
Example: The test was difficult. Nevertheless, most students passed.
Watch out: however links sentences, not clauses: It was late. However, we stayed, not It was late however we stayed.', '[{"label":"When to use","text":"To show a contrast or an unexpected result between two sentences."},{"label":"Key difference","text":"however / nevertheless = an unexpected result; in contrast / on the other hand = a comparison of two things."},{"label":"Example","text":"The test was difficult. Nevertheless, most students passed."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"however links sentences, not clauses: It was late. However, we stayed, not It was late however we stayed."}]', 2526);
INSERT INTO feedback_items (feedback_code, test_id, test_part_id, question_id, kind, type_ref, heading, body, fields_json, sort_order) VALUES ('01-01-05-025-27-00', 1, 1, (SELECT id FROM questions WHERE question_code = '01-01-05-025-27'), 'micro_feedback', 'A05-T25', 'Having + past participle (Having finished…)', 'When to use: To show that one action was completed before the next one.
Look for: two actions in sequence by the same person.
Example: Having finished her homework, she went out to play.
Watch out: Use Having done, not Had done or the base verb: Having reviewed the notes, he felt ready, not Had reviewed the notes, he felt ready.', '[{"label":"When to use","text":"To show that one action was completed before the next one."},{"label":"Look for","text":"two actions in sequence by the same person."},{"label":"Example","text":"Having finished her homework, she went out to play."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"Use Having done, not Had done or the base verb: Having reviewed the notes, he felt ready, not Had reviewed the notes, he felt ready."}]', 2527);
INSERT INTO feedback_items (feedback_code, test_id, test_part_id, question_id, kind, type_ref, heading, body, fields_json, sort_order) VALUES ('01-01-05-025-28-00', 1, 1, (SELECT id FROM questions WHERE question_code = '01-01-05-025-28'), 'micro_feedback', 'A05-T20', 'Adding information (moreover, furthermore, in addition)', 'When to use: To add another point that supports what you have just said.
Look for: a second sentence that adds a similar or stronger point.
Example: The hotel was cheap. Moreover, it was close to the beach.
Watch out: Do not join two sentences with only a comma: It was cheap. Moreover, it was close to the beach, not It was cheap, moreover it was close to the beach.', '[{"label":"When to use","text":"To add another point that supports what you have just said."},{"label":"Look for","text":"a second sentence that adds a similar or stronger point."},{"label":"Example","text":"The hotel was cheap. Moreover, it was close to the beach."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"Do not join two sentences with only a comma: It was cheap. Moreover, it was close to the beach, not It was cheap, moreover it was close to the beach."}]', 2528);
