Watch out: You may keep the present for facts that are always true: He said that the earth revolves around the sun.', '[{"label":"When to use","text":"When the reported words are a scientific fact, a general truth or something still true."},{"label":"Look for","text":"facts about nature, science or permanent habits."},{"label":"Example","text":"The teacher said that water boils at 100°C."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"You may keep the present for facts that are always true: He said that the earth revolves around the sun."}]', 3107);
INSERT INTO feedback_items (feedback_code, test_id, test_part_id, question_id, kind, type_ref, heading, body, fields_json, sort_order) VALUES ('01-01-07-031-08-00', 1, 1, (SELECT id FROM questions WHERE question_code = '01-01-07-031-08'), 'micro_feedback', 'A07-T04', 'Reported speech: changing modals (can, may, must, shall)', 'When to use: When the original words contain a modal verb.
Look for: can → could; may → might; must → had to; shall → would / should.
Example: ‘I can''t find my phone.’ → He said he couldn''t find his phone.
Watch out: Change can, may and must, but leave could, might, should and would as they are.', '[{"label":"When to use","text":"When the original words contain a modal verb."},{"label":"Look for","text":"can → could; may → might; must → had to; shall → would / should."},{"label":"Example","text":"‘I can''t find my phone.’ → He said he couldn''t find his phone."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"Change can, may and must, but leave could, might, should and would as they are."}]', 3108);
