Look for: a past participle (Exhausted, Frightened, Surrounded) at the start, followed by the person or thing affected.
Example: Frightened by the thunder, the dog hid under the bed.
Watch out: Use the past participle when the subject is affected: Exhausted by the trek, the hikers rested, not Exhausting by the trek.', '[{"label":"When to use","text":"To describe a state or feeling that happened to the subject (passive meaning)."},{"label":"Look for","text":"a past participle (Exhausted, Frightened, Surrounded) at the start, followed by the person or thing affected."},{"label":"Example","text":"Frightened by the thunder, the dog hid under the bed."},{"label":"Watch out","text":"Use the past participle when the subject is affected: Exhausted by the trek, the hikers rested, not Exhausting by the trek."}]', 5201);
